6. Overall Activity

The investigation identified a sequence of firewall configuration changes:

Create firewall rule → Allow inbound port 450 → Registry modification → Delete firewall rule

The netsh utility was used to create and subsequently remove the firewall rule. The associated registry modification provides evidence that the Windows Firewall configuration was changed during the activity.

7. MITRE ATT&CK Mapping

Tactic: Defense Evasion

Relevant Technique:
T1562.004 – Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify System Firewall

The activity is relevant to this technique because the firewall configuration was modified to permit inbound network traffic through port 450.

8. Investigation Conclusion

The observed activity demonstrates that a firewall rule named atomic testing was created using netsh to allow inbound traffic on port 450. A corresponding registry modification was observed, confirming changes to the firewall configuration. The same rule was later deleted using netsh.

The complete sequence indicates a temporary firewall configuration change, potentially allowing network communication during the activity before the rule was removed.

Final Activity Summary:

netsh was used to create a firewall rule named atomic testing, allowing inbound traffic on port 450. Registry modifications related to the firewall configuration were subsequently observed, and the same firewall rule was later deleted. The activity is associated with Defense Evasion through modification of the system firewall configuration.
